    "body": "@domenic OK, here is my test case:\r\n```\r\n<html>\r\n  <body></body>\r\n  <script>\r\n    var child = document.createElement(\"iframe\");\r\n    child.src = \"about:blank\";\r\n    child.onload = function() {\r\n      var child1 = child.contentDocument;\r\n      child1.write('Child loaded. <a href=\"data:text/html,Check the console!\">Click me!</a>');\r\n      var grandchild = child1.createElement(\"iframe\");\r\n      grandchild.src = \"empty.html\";\r\n      grandchild.onload = function() {\r\n        var grandchild1 = grandchild.contentDocument;\r\n        grandchild1.write('Grandchild loaded');\r\n        child.onload = function() {\r\n          // This is a write to an active but not fully active document\r\n          grandchild1.write(' WRITE HAPPENED');\r\n          console.log(grandchild1.body.innerText);\r\n          // This is a write to an inactive document\r\n          child1.write(' WRITE HAPPENED');\r\n          console.log(child1.body.innerText);\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n      child1.body.appendChild(grandchild);\r\n    }\r\n    document.body.appendChild(child);\r\n  </script>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn FF it does what the spec says, the write to the child fails but the write to the grandchild succeeds.\r\n\r\nIn Chrome, both writes succeed.\r\n\r\nI'm on a Linux box, so I can't test Safari or Edge.",
